   "title": {
    "en": "Different responses"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Zitkala-Sa resists physically and is overpowered; her resistance shows courage but she is helpless as a child. Bama turns her anger into a long-term plan: to study and earn respect. Both later became writers who exposed injustice, so their writing itself is a form of resistance. The values are equality, dignity for all and education as empowerment."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "Zitkala-Sa: immediate, physical resistance"
    },
    {
     "en": "Bama: education as resistance"
    },
    {
     "en": "Writing itself as protest"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "Zitkala-Sa resists physically and is overpowered; her resistance shows courage but she is helpless as a child. Bama turns her anger into a long-term plan: to study and earn respect. Both later became writers who exposed injustice, so their writing itself is a form of resistance. The values are equality, dignity for all and education as empowerment."
